{ id:"d2-34", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A Claude Sonnet 5 workload mixes trivial lookups with hard planning requests. Which thinking configuration best balances quality, latency, and cost?",
  options:["Use one manual token budget sized for the hardest requests","Disable thinking globally to make every response equally fast","Change effort on every turn within the same cached conversation","Use adaptive thinking at an effort level selected by workload evals"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Sonnet 5 supports adaptive thinking, while effort steers how often and how deeply it reasons. Workload evals should select the level: lower settings can skip thinking on simple turns, while higher settings favor deeper reasoning. A uses a deprecated model-dependent control, B sacrifices hard-task quality, and C can invalidate cached prefixes when effort changes." },
{ id:"d2-35", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A candidate model cuts cost by 40% and passes aggregate accuracy, but raises a critical false-negative rate above the rollback threshold declared before testing. What should the team do?",
  options:["Stop the rollout because the predeclared critical-error threshold was breached","Continue because aggregate accuracy and cost targets were both met","Redefine the threshold using the candidate's observed result","Replace the task eval with a broader public benchmark comparison"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"A predeclared rollback threshold prevents aggregate gains from hiding unacceptable critical failures. B ignores the binding risk metric, C changes success criteria after seeing the result, and D substitutes a generic benchmark for the production task." },
{ id:"d2-36", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"core", source:"generated",
  question:"A reviewer worries that prompt caching will subtly change response quality. Which statement is accurate?",
  options:["Caching replays prior completions whenever the prompt prefix matches","Caching reuses prompt processing without intentionally changing response behavior","Caching lowers input cost by accepting a small quality loss","Caching is reliable only when generation uses deterministic sampling"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Prompt caching stores and reuses processed prompt content, not prior completions. It does not introduce a quality trade-off, although two separately generated responses can still vary. A describes response caching, while C and D invent restrictions that prompt caching does not impose." }
